DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ddff prints the amount of free space on one or more file sys-
     tems.  Each named argument may be a block- or character-
     special file (the raw or block file system device), or an
     ordinary directory, in which case ddff looks up the device
     that directory resides on by searching /ddeevv for a special
     file with a matching device number.

     With no arguments, ddff reports on the file system that holds
     the current directory (unless -aa is given alone, in which
     case it reports only on the mounted file systems, as below).
     With one or more arguments, it reports on each in turn.

     For each file system, ddff prints its name, then the number of
     free blocks over the total number of blocks (excluding the
     space used by the i-list), and that fraction again as a per-
     centage.

     -aa  Also report on every file system currently listed in
         /eettcc/mmnnttttaabb (or, if that does not exist, in the older
         /eettcc/mmttaabb format), in addition to any _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y or
         _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m arguments given.

     -ii  Also print the number of free i-nodes over the total
         number of i-nodes, and that fraction as a percentage.

     -tt  Also print the total number of blocks on the device.

